# Repository & Git safety

- Work from a normal local clone. If the checkout lives inside a syncing folder
  (iCloud Drive, Dropbox, etc.), STOP and report a relocation plan first.
- Apply the user's current task and standing grants within their defined scope. Authorized
  routine commits, batched pushes, isolated branches/worktrees, PR updates and reviewed
  feature integration need no repeated phase approval. Main merge, deployment, destructive
  history changes and external mutations retain separate authority and release gates.
- Do not reset, force-push, delete branches, drop stashes, rewrite history or perform other
  Git mutations outside the existing task grant.
- Preserve all existing files and uncommitted work. Never discard local changes.
- Inspect (`git status`, diff) before changing anything. Prefer minimal, scoped diffs.
- Never modify autogenerated Alembic migration files by hand.
- `.ai/` and `.cursor/rules/` are version-controlled governance (safe to track), except the
  ignored `.ai/local/` and `.ai/private/` subtrees (machine-local / sensitive material).
- Treat `HANDOFF.md`, `CHANGELOG_SESSION.md`, `*.local.md`, `.ai/local/`, and `.ai/private/`
  as gitignored for ordinary product commits; never stage them into product PRs.
  Exception (see `.cursor/rules/70-handoff.mdc`): on a dedicated `cursor/*handoff*` branch,
  force-add and push only `HANDOFF.md` + `CHANGELOG_SESSION.md` so the Mac cloud handoff
  sync can discover them — and do not open a PR for that handoff-only branch.
- Before authorized Git writes, inspect the owned diff, exclude unrelated work, check secrets
  and run relevant verification. Missing mobile sync does not gate code publication. Pause
  only an actually unauthorized operation and prepare concrete evidence before escalation.
